WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.890
Abraham: Cyber security professionals are
always on the lookout for new threats and

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:05.819
use many different sources to find them.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:10.360
By combining information from different
sources, a better picture can be seen.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:12.889
Let’s look at some of
the sources they use.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:17.530
Nicole: Indicators of Compromise
are pieces of forensic data that

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:21.550
serve as evidence of potential
intrusions on a system or network

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:26.910
and may be unusual traffic, unknown
files, or suspicious activity.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:31.859
These enable information security
professionals and system administrators

00:00:32.099 --> 00:00:34.000
to detect intrusion attempts.

00:00:34.559 --> 00:00:38.280
This information can offer actionable
threat intelligence that can be shared

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:40.169
with the cyber security community.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.440
Abraham: Below the surface of the
‘open web’ we all use, lies the

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:49.069
Deep, and Dark web and these are not
accessible by standard search engines.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:53.300
This part of the internet hides someone’s
identity and location and is only

00:00:53.309 --> 00:00:55.239
accessible through specialist browsers.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:01:00.000
The dark web is a channel for all
kinds of illegal activities and

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.680
cyber criminals use them to purchase
hacking tools and stolen data.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.690
Nicole: Social media platforms are
a conduit for cyber attacks as data

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:10.959
sharing is an inherent part of the apps.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.020
They provide a ready resource of
personal data which is meant to be

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:19.500
shared between family and friends but can
instead be accessed by cyber criminals.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.440
Privacy settings are in place, but most
users don’t give these a second glance.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:29.239
Phishing is common on social media
and fake accounts are easy to set up.

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:34.490
Abraham: Messaging platforms are used
by threat actors to communicate as well

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:36.750
as finding out about data leaks etc.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:41.580
Chat networks are used in a number of ways
by cyber criminals for advertising their

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:46.750
products and services such as cashing
out, carding and crypto currency fraud.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:52.589
Nicole: Cyber attacks and cyber offensive
capabilities, are blurring the lines

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:54.810
between spies and malicious hackers.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.000
Nation state and non-state actors are
reshaping the cyber threat landscape.

00:02:00.539 --> 00:02:05.069
Threat actors can inject high levels
of geopolitical instability and

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.660
some nations use cyber espionage and
criminality as a tool, to destabilise or

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:13.209
gain economic or commercial advantage.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.030
Current geopolitical tensions are
constantly monitored to offer clues of

00:02:18.059 --> 00:02:20.420
who might be targeted in the next attack.

00:02:21.139 --> 00:02:24.680
Abraham: Malware analysis can help to
show indicators of compromise to look for

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.250
as signs of a system breach it cand also
give an insight into the latest tactics

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:31.690
and techniques be used by threat actors.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:37.960
Nicole: Exploit databases are archives of
public exploits and vulnerable software.

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:43.130
They are developed by ‘pentesters’ and
‘vulnerability researchers’ and give

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:47.810
insights into which exploits are available
to threat actors and which vulnerabilities

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:49.950
need to be prioritised for patching.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:55.140
Abraham: Paste sites are sites where users
can share plain text blocks as a link.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:59.839
These can be used innocently but more
often than not, are used to leak breached

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:05.960
data, source codes, links to dark web
sites or targets of upcoming attacks.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:10.660
Nicole: Information sharing platforms
are sites that provide information

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:12.770
about current threat actor activities.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:17.240
They may be government funded, or
independent, and include the UK

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:21.040
‘National Cyber Security Centre’s
Cyber Security Information Sharing

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.970
Partnership’ and the ‘Financial Services
Information Sharing and Analysis Centre’.

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:29.750
For more information on these and
how cyber professionals use their

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.089
services, check out the weblinks below.

